ese films and shows for bucky's  missing arm to be forgotten about as  being a disability since the prosthetic  works perfectly 99.9 of the time oh i'll  get that up  now again this isn't all automatically  bad much like with not having  accommodations like ramps how that says  something about the world you've built  so too does having the kind of  technology or assistance that basically  removes the disability altogether in  this case it says that this world  doesn't just leave those with  disabilities to fend for themselves and  accommodates them far better than we do  however  it's addressing a problem by  fixing a disabled person so that they  can function in a world not built for  them rather than altering the greater  world so that their condition isn't that  disruptive to their lives in the first  place  just something to keep in mind  but we are going to move on because i  kind of touched on something there that  i want to dive further into  [Applause]  now this is different from disability as  character motivation because it's less  about forcing the decisions of the  specific disabled character and more  about shifting the plot as a whole so  remember two paragraphs ago when i said  for all intents and purposes bucky  doesn't really have a disability until  the writers decide to do something with  it well  this has a more common form found in all  genres of entertainment not just sci-fi  and fantasy now what we're about to talk  about is most commonly associated with  invisible disability so to quickly touch  on that an invisible disability is one  where you wouldn't know that a person  has a disability just by looking at them  because a lot of what we've talked about  so far you can basically spot with the  naked eye either by evidence of the  disability itself or by the visibility  of an assisting device  invisible disabilities often come in the  form of chronic conditions like diabetes  lupus fibromyalgia  ibs even food allergies  things that impact the people with these  conditions but other people wouldn't  know about unless they were told  these kinds of conditions partly due to  their invisible nature are prime  candidates for what i've come to think  of as plot convenient titus  this is a condition that is never  addressed except for when it can be used  to either force plot progression or be a  sudden complication to enhance drama  it's almost never just a background  element that the character is dealing  with it's almost always either totally  under control or a life-threateningly  urgent issue  the character who caused me to  start thinking about and using this term  was kasima from orphan black  now while it's certainly not without  merit and was a hell of an acting  showcase for its lead i've got  a lot of problems uh with orphan black  and we don't have time to get into all  of them but talking specifically about  kasima she suffers from an unspecified  condition that basically means she  coughs up blood when it's dramatically  appropriate  and can then force the other characters  to change whatever course they're on in  order to help her  but she's fine the rest of the time  whenever the plot doesn't need that  now i don't want to be overly dismissive  here  i'm not saying that the mere fact that  the condition flares up and comes down  is itself the issue there are plenty of  real world conditions that do the exact  same thing though as with freddie  freeman that i mentioned earlier the  creators opted for the safety of not  naming a real world condition  but ultimately  what i again a non-disabled person so  take what i'm about to say with a grain  of salt what i found irritating was that  the entire thing felt more like a means  to jolt the story whenever it stalled  rather than an actual aspect of her  character there is a fairly fine line  between depicting the difficulties of a  chronic condition  and using that condition as an on-call  plot defibrillator and it's a line i  personally feel or from black crossed  plot convenient titus can do everything  from remove a character who no longer  serves a plot function to simply being a  complication that ups the dramatic  stakes of the climax to serving as a  moment of triumph should the character  ever overcome it basically a mini  version of triumph over adversity  without making it the whole thing  but the point is  it's there to serve those plot functions  more than any amount of representation  or characterization that's why despite  my earlier examples of specific  invisible disabilities it's pretty  common for these to be unspecified as  was the case with kasima it doesn't  matter what the disability is  if it's just a plot device in the end  but there's  okay there's kind of a big aspect to all  this and  okay frankly i've been putting it off  until now  [Laughter]  now the reason i've been putting this  off is because it's possibly the most  loaded part of an already loaded topic  but yes there is a  long-running issue of disabilities  especially visible ones and especially  especially disfiguring ones being given  to villainous characters and this one  goes  way back from modern instances like  shadow weavers severe facial scarring  and shira and the princess of power to  dr knows prosthetic hands from the first  james bond movie to long john silver's  missing leg and treasure island to the  titular character's physical deformities  in shakespeare's richard iii  from twisted monstrosities and  adult-aimed horror films like mason  verger from hannibal to children's fair  like captain hook from peter pan or  howard clifford and detective pikachu  it's a fairly standard and  straightforward thing  to make a character instill a sense of  dread or unease in the audience  twist the human form into something  recognizable but not immediately  familiar  it's actually not that far off from a  deliberate evocation of the uncanny  valley effect where people get unsettled  when they know what they're looking at  is a person but something's off  something's not  what they expect  and  on paper  that should be fine the problem however  is that this reshaping of the human form  frequently perfectly mirrors real world  physical conditions actually i'd say it  does that about 99.9 of the time and the  thing is even positive characters with  disfiguring disabilities are often  introduced with the expectation that the  hero or at least the audience is wary  and put off by them just so that  expectation can be subverted a little  bit later this is basically the entire  gag behind scenes like the snuggly  duckling pub entangled but despite my  evil look and my temper and my heart  i've always yearned to be a concert  pianist  the joke only works on the base  assumption that the guy with the hook  for a hand is going to be perceived as a  threat by the audience until he suddenly  flips that on its head which has the  classic problem of such humor  in that yes it is subverting the trope  but only after evoking it as hard as  possible  and here's where we're going to circle  back around to what we brought up before  about disability driving character  motivation because  this is not only frequently the case  with disabled villains but in a  frightening amount of cases the  disability can actually be said to be  the cause of their villainy  a few quick examples  i mentioned howard clifford earlier  whose desperate attempts to find a cure  for his worsening physical condition  again never specifically naming it at  any point leads him down a path of  enslavement and forced body augmentation  and attempted murder john lumick from  series two of modern doctor who created  a version of the cybermen with the  ultimate goal of preserving his own mind  before his unspecified illness claims  his life now with characters like lumic  and clifford one might make the argument  that it's more a fear of death that's  driving these characters than their  disabilities  and there's some truth to that  but the storytellers are still choosing  to embody that fear of death in a  disabled person and one could equally  argue that their conditions made them  far more aware of their mortality and  thus push them further into villainy  than might have otherwise happened had  their disabilities not made them more  aware of it at the very least you have  to grant that their disabilities are  exacerbating the fears that are driving  their villainous actions  there isn't always an extrapolated fear  of dying though we already talked about  kirk connors aka the lizard and his  missing arm  missing arms seem to be a bit of a go-to  actually captain hook claw from black  panther then there's the albino orc from  the hobbit movies  actually albinism is also a go-to the  condition being seen either in villains  or uh occasionally characters with  effectively magic powers like caliban  and logan or powder  actually  since i started dipping back into comic  book examples back there i'd be remiss  to not bring up batman's rogues gallery  now it's well documented that many of  the key figures from batman's villains  are suffering from severe psychological  issues hence why they keep ending up in  an asylum but physical disability or  disfigurement abounds regardless the  obvious example being two-face who's  physical and psychological issues are  pretty intrinsically linked but there's  also the penguin whose body is misshapen  in a number of ways that tend to vary  from depiction to depiction but is  nearly always present in some form from  the misshapen hands given to him in  batman returns to the persistent limp he  had in gotham the character of killer  croc tends to be more depicted as a flat  out monster these days but when he was  first introduced he was basically just a  big guy with a skin condition now i've  started to touch on disfiguring  conditions and disabilities and this  aspect bears a little bit of a highlight  because while heroic characters aren't  above the occasional scar it's generally  going to be  or something cool  a cut across the cheek a nick that gives  them that segmented eyebrow look that  the kids really like these days  generally a whole bunch of single scars  carefully placed to ensure minimal  effect on the character's appearance  i mean there's a reason that the tv  version of gerion from game of thrones  only suffered a single cut across the  bridge of the nose and the cheek as a  result of the battle of black water  while book tyrion has been missing about  half his nose since then  anything that seriously reshapes the  face can be counted on as being a trait  reserved for villains be it silco and  arcane dr poison from wonder woman  affiliates from 300 heck most of the  entire persian army in 300 and countless  james bond villains seriously the james  bond franchise for all its supposed  reinvention over the years has never  been able to stop  doing this  heroes almost never have these kind of  severe or prominent disfigurements and  the only two prominent examples that i  can think of deadpool and jonah hex fall  firmly into the category of anti-hero  not that there's anything wrong with  anti-heroes but it kind of feels like an  admission that this kind of physical  state would never be granted to a  paragon type hero or anything close to  that  the pattern is pretty clear  if audiences can see something that is  clearly  wrong  with the character's body then the odds  of them being anything other than a  villain  are slim to none  most of the time this is where i would  lay out some examples of times when  movies or shows got it right  i'm not gonna do that this time not  because there aren't any but rather  because of my own status  being outside the disabled community i  don't feel comfortable with my ability  to identify the good instances i've got  some things that i think are good  examples but i am too aware of my own  blind spots to trust that i won't  accidentally cite examples that actually  have problems i haven't even considered  and didn't even talk about  so yeah we're going to be skipping over  that part this time and it can serve as  a reminder of what i said at the  beginning that while i speak  with the best of intentions i am  speaking as an outsider and i may well  have missed something important in all  of this  mine is not a voice you should be  prioritizing over actual people with  disabilities  but  i did want to talk about these issues  all the same  as best i could  and hopefully i didn't screw it up too  badly  i'm sure folks will let me know in the  comments either way  but  that's where i'm going to leave off  so what are your thoughts on the  patterns i've discussed what you think  might be a more positive examples just  remember to be respectful not just of me  but of other commenters as well  and  put your thoughts down in the comments  and let's talk about it  if you want to support what this channel  does and the kind of content i create i  have a patreon that is how i pay my  bills this is my living  any assistance is greatly appreciated  but in addition to that down in the  description you will find links to well  my book which i mentioned earlier but  also other things that i do  other platforms i'm on be it twitch tick  tock twitter etc  but um  don't feel too much pressure though  at the end of the day  you are the council  i am just running the meetings  and until next time this council  is adjourned  [Music]  patreon shout outs thank you to turok  shane ross oliver b melinda walters  robin powell for nobulax the poodle  edelon tracy scrabbit dharma michael  doyle vincent paul bartolucci angry  casper adam lefty taylor robin moore  shayla gourley and bookworm  you can support me as well  hear me mispronounce your name  sure i got some of those wrong  [Music]  um  [Music]
 

